
== research: reading about the topic on the web
  | **HOW IT WORKS**
  | 1. An IR LED sends out weak infrared pulses.
  | 2. The beam is too weak to bounce back from the floor, so the receiver sees almost nothing.
  | 3. A hand reflects the IR back into the receiver beside the LED.
  | 4. The control board compares the signal with a threshold. Some models have a trimmer pot for sensitivity.
  | 5. If the signal is above the threshold, the board switches on the fan and heater.
  | 6. When the hand leaves, the reflection drops and the fan stops. Some dryers also stop at the end of a timed cycle.
  | 
  | **REAL PARTS**
  | - IR LED plus an IR photodiode or phototransistor receiver (the sources describe this pair, not specific part numbers).
  | - A sensitivity trimmer pot (from the Supreme manual).
  | - Dyson Airblade Tap: IR sensors plus the Dyson digital motor V4.
  | - I did not verify a comparator part such as the LM393. If you mention it, call it "typical".
  | 
  | **MYTH**
  | - Myth: the dryer "sees" your hand or senses heat.
  | - Truth: most designs emit their own IR and detect its reflection. Some designs only receive IR that hands give off.
  | 
  | **SURPRISING FACT**
  | - Mirrors, polished steel and glossy tile can keep the sensor triggered.
  | - Sunlight can also cause activation when nobody is there.
  | - The only number I found is a vendor rule of thumb: after cleaning, a dryer that shuts off in 1–2 seconds had a dirty sensor. I found no other verified numbers.
  | 
  | **TIP**
  | - If the dryer runs by itself or won't start, clean the sensor window with a soft cloth.
  | 
  | Sources: [Naked Scientists](https://www.thenakedscientists.com/node/179908), [Supreme Airforce manual](https://media.cetnaj.com.au/Installation/Supreme/Supreme_Airforce.pdf), [ProDryers troubleshooting](https://www.prodryers.com/blog/commercial-hand-dryer-troubleshooting-guide-6-common-problems-expert-fixes-preventive-maintenance/), [FMLink on Dyson Airblade Tap](https://www.fmlink.com/dyson-airblade-tap-hand-dryer-washes-and-dries-hands-at-the-sink/)

